is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I validated both floor VLANs (10.5.0.x and 10.6.0.x) end-to-end and found them operating normally. Clients can reach their default gateways: pc1 (10.5.0.1) pings 10.5.0.254 with 0% loss and sub‑ms RTT, and pc3 (10.6.0.3) pings 10.6.0.254 likewise. Cross-VLAN reachability is also good (e.g., multiple hosts successfully ping 172.16.30.12 per the topology-wide tests), which implies upstream routing is intact.

On the Campus 2 access switch (switch4), the VLAN L2 domains are correctly built:
- br_vlan5 members: eth1, eth2, eth3, eth6 and trunk subif eth0.5
- br_vlan6 members: eth4, eth5 and trunk subif eth0.6
On the distribution side (sshswitch), the trunk carries VLANs 5 and 6 correctly:
- br_v5 members: bond0.5 and eth0.5; IP 10.5.0.254/24 present
- br_v6 members: bond0.6 and eth0.6; IP 10.6.0.254/24 present
OSPF on sshswitch is up via 55.5.1.2, and routes to other subnets are installed, confirming upstream reachability.

Mechanistically, if a VLAN had “fallen off the trunk,” hosts would be unable to ARP their gateway; we would see gateway pings fail. Instead, ARP/L2 and L3 paths are good now. Localization: no current fault on switch4.eth0↔sshswitch.eth0 trunk or the VLAN bridges. Root cause: no persistent misconfiguration found; likely a transient condition during the maintenance window that has since been corrected. Corrective fix: none needed now. Recommend locking trunk config (eth0.5/eth0.6 on both ends), saving changes, and enabling monitoring/LLDP to catch any future trunk tagging regressions. 
=== END ===